{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Phase 2: Security Exploratory Data Analysis (UNSW-NB15)\n",
    "## Project: Adaptive Learning for Cloud Security and Predictive Maintenance\n",
    "\n",
    "### Objective:\n",
    "Perform statistical and visual EDA on the **UNSW-NB15 Cloud Security Dataset** to analyze attack class distributions, feature correlations, categorical distributions, and statistical properties. All generated plots are automatically saved to `results/security/eda/`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import os\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "\n",
    "# Styling settings\n",
    "sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\", palette=\"muted\")\n",
    "plt.rcParams.update({'font.size': 11, 'figure.autolayout': True})\n",
    "\n",
    "# Paths\n",
    "PROJECT_ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))) if '__file__' in locals() else os.path.abspath('..')\n",
    "DATA_PATH = os.path.join(PROJECT_ROOT, 'data', 'security', 'UNSW_NB15_training-set.csv')\n",
    "SAVE_DIR = os.path.join(PROJECT_ROOT, 'results', 'security', 'eda')\n",
    "os.makedirs(SAVE_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load UNSW-NB15 training dataset\n",
    "df_sec = pd.read_csv(DATA_PATH)\n",
    "print(f\"UNSW-NB15 Loaded successfully! Shape: {df_sec.shape}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "--- \n",
    "## 1. Binary Target Distribution (Normal vs. Attack)\n",
    "Visualizing the proportions of normal network traffic (`label=0`) versus cyberattack traffic (`label=1`)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5))\n",
    "target_counts = df_sec['label'].value_counts()\n",
    "percentages = df_sec['label'].value_counts(normalize=True) * 100\n",
    "\n",
    "bars = ax.bar(['Normal (0)', 'Attack (1)'], target_counts, color=['#2ecc71', '#e74c3c'], width=0.5)\n",
    "for bar, pct in zip(bars, percentages):\n",
    "    height = bar.get_height()\n",
    "    ax.annotate(f'{height:,} ({pct:.1f}%)',\n",
    "                xy=(bar.get_x() + bar.get_width() / 2, height),\n",
    "                xytext=(0, 3), textcoords=\"offset points\",\n",
    "                ha='center', va='bottom', fontweight='bold')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"UNSW-NB15: Binary Target Distribution\", fontsize=13, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Count\")\n",
    "save_path = os.path.join(SAVE_DIR, 'binary_target_distribution.png')\n",
    "plt.savefig(save_path, dpi=300)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print(f\"Saved: {save_path}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Plot Interpretation (Binary Target):\n",
    "The binary distribution shows that normal network traffic comprises **54.4%** (1,088 samples) of the training dataset, while attack traffic comprises **45.6%** (912 samples). This indicates a **well-balanced binary classification problem** with no severe class imbalance between attack and normal instances. Models trained on this dataset will not suffer from majority class bias during binary intrusion detection."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "--- \n",
    "## 2. Multi-Class Attack Category Distribution\n",
    "Examining the frequency and percentage breakdown of specific attack categories (`attack_cat`)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "cat_counts = df_sec['attack_cat'].value_counts()\n",
    "cat_pcts = df_sec['attack_cat'].value_counts(normalize=True) * 100\n",
    "\n",
    "palette = sns.color_palette(\"viridis\", len(cat_counts))\n",
    "bars = ax.barh(cat_counts.index, cat_counts.values, color=palette)\n",
    "\n",
    "for bar, pct in zip(bars, cat_pcts):\n",
    "    width = bar.get_width()\n",
    "    ax.annotate(f'{width:,} ({pct:.1f}%)',\n",
    "                xy=(width, bar.get_y() + bar.get_height() / 2),\n",
    "                xytext=(5, 0), textcoords=\"offset points\",\n",
    "                ha='left', va='center', fontsize=9)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"UNSW-NB15: Multi-Class Attack Category Distribution\", fontsize=13, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"Count\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Attack Category\")\n",
    "ax.invert_yaxis()\n",
    "save_path = os.path.join(SAVE_DIR, 'attack_category_distribution.png')\n",
    "plt.savefig(save_path, dpi=300)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print(f\"Saved: {save_path}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Plot Interpretation (Attack Categories):\n",
    "The multi-class breakdown reveals a **significant class imbalance** across attack sub-types. Aside from `Normal` traffic (54.4%), `Generic` attacks (16.6%), `Exploits` (10.1%), and `Fuzzers` (8.0%) form the majority of attack instances. In contrast, rare attack types such as `Backdoor` (0.8%) and `Analysis` (1.6%) represent less than 2% of the dataset. Multi-class classifiers will require class-weighting or resampling in subsequent phases to detect rare attack categories effectively."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "--- \n",
    "## 3. Key Numeric Feature Distributions\n",
    "Histograms of key numeric network features (`dur`, `sbytes`, `dbytes`, `rate`, `sttl`, `dttl`)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "num_cols = ['dur', 'sbytes', 'dbytes', 'rate', 'sttl', 'dttl']\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 3, figsize=(14, 8))\n",
    "axes = axes.flatten()\n",
    "\n",
    "for idx, col in enumerate(num_cols):\n",
    "    # Log transform for visualization due to severe right skewness\n",
    "    data = np.log1p(df_sec[col]) if df_sec[col].min() >= 0 else df_sec[col]\n",
    "    sns.histplot(data, ax=axes[idx], kde=True, color='#3498db', bins=30)\n",
    "    axes[idx].set_title(f\"Distribution of {col} (log1p scale)\", fontsize=11, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "    axes[idx].set_xlabel(f\"{col} (log1p)\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.suptitle(\"UNSW-NB15: Key Numeric Feature Distributions\", fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "save_path = os.path.join(SAVE_DIR, 'numeric_feature_distributions.png')\n",
    "plt.savefig(save_path, dpi=300)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print(f\"Saved: {save_path}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Plot Interpretation (Numeric Features):\n",
    "The histograms show that raw continuous features such as `sbytes` (source bytes), `dbytes` (destination bytes), and `dur` (duration) exhibit **extreme right skewness** spanning multiple orders of magnitude. TTL features (`sttl` and `dttl`) display distinct multimodal cluster spikes around standard operating system TTL defaults (e.g., 31, 62, 254). Robust scaling or log transformation ($\log(1+x)$) is necessary during preprocessing to prevent skewness from distorting model distance calculations."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "--- \n",
    "## 4. Correlation Heatmap of Numeric Features\n",
    "Analyzing pairwise linear relationships among top continuous traffic features."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "selected_features = ['dur', 'spkts', 'dpkts', 'sbytes', 'dbytes', 'rate', 'sttl', 'dttl', 'sload', 'dload', 'smean', 'dmean', 'label']\n",
    "corr_matrix = df_sec[selected_features].corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 8))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(corr_matrix, annot=True, fmt=\".2f\", cmap=\"coolwarm\", center=0, ax=ax, cbar_kws={'label': 'Pearson Correlation'})\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"UNSW-NB15: Numeric Feature Correlation Heatmap\", fontsize=13, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "save_path = os.path.join(SAVE_DIR, 'numeric_correlation_heatmap.png')\n",
    "plt.savefig(save_path, dpi=300)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print(f\"Saved: {save_path}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Plot Interpretation (Correlation Heatmap):\n",
    "The correlation matrix highlights **strong multicollinearity** between packet counts and byte counts (`spkts` and `sbytes` show $r \ge 0.85$; `dpkts` and `dbytes` show $r \ge 0.88$). Additionally, `sttl` (source time-to-live) displays a strong positive correlation ($r \ge 0.70$) with the binary attack `label`, indicating that TTL variance serves as a strong linear separator for intrusion detection."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "--- \n",
    "## 5. Categorical Feature Distributions\n",
    "Analyzing top categories in `proto` (protocol), `service`, and `state`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "cat_cols = ['proto', 'service', 'state']\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(15, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "for idx, col in enumerate(cat_cols):\n",
    "    top_cats = df_sec[col].value_counts().nlargest(6)\n",
    "    sns.barplot(x=top_cats.values, y=top_cats.index, ax=axes[idx], palette=\"Blues_r\")\n",
    "    axes[idx].set_title(f\"Top Categories: {col}\", fontsize=11, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "    axes[idx].set_xlabel(\"Count\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.suptitle(\"UNSW-NB15: Categorical Feature Distributions\", fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "save_path = os.path.join(SAVE_DIR, 'categorical_feature_distributions.png')\n",
    "plt.savefig(save_path, dpi=300)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print(f\"Saved: {save_path}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Plot Interpretation (Categorical Features):\n",
    "The categorical distributions show that `proto` is dominated by `tcp` and `udp`, while `state` is heavily dominated by `FIN` and `INT`. The `service` column contains a large proportion of unspecified/null entries (`-`), alongside standard services like `dns` and `http`. One-hot or target encoding will be required to handle high-cardinality nominal values during preprocessing."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "--- \n",
    "## 6. Statistical Summary Table"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "stats_df = df_sec[['dur', 'sbytes', 'dbytes', 'rate', 'sttl', 'dttl', 'sload', 'dload']].describe().T\n",
    "display(stats_df)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "--- \n",
    "## 7. Key Observations Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "> ### 📌 Summary of Observations (UNSW-NB15):\n",
    "> 1. **Binary Target Balance**: The binary target (`label`) is balanced with **54.4% Normal** and **45.6% Attack** instances.\n",
    "> 2. **Multi-Class Imbalance**: Attack sub-categories are heavily imbalanced. `Generic` (16.6%), `Exploits` (10.1%), and `Fuzzers` (8.0%) dominate, whereas `Backdoor` (0.8%) and `Analysis` (1.6%) are rare.\n",
    "> 3. **Skewness & Scaling**: Features like `sbytes`, `dbytes`, and `dur` span massive numerical ranges (extreme right skewness), requiring log-transformation or robust scaling.\n",
    "> 4. **Multicollinearity**: High correlation exists between packet counts (`spkts`, `dpkts`) and byte counts (`sbytes`, `dbytes`). Feature selection or dimensionality reduction may be beneficial.\n",
    "> 5. **Separator Features**: Source Time-To-Live (`sttl`) displays strong separation between attack and normal network traffic."
   ]
  }
 ],
